# 18. Baseline Conclusion

The United States enters the next phase of conservation policy with neither a blank slate nor a finished system.

It possesses:

* enormous protected-land assets;
* sophisticated scientific institutions;
* extensive wildlife and fisheries programs;
* substantial state and Tribal conservation capacity;
* established funding mechanisms;
* decades of accumulated ecological knowledge; and
* numerous examples of successful conservation.

It also faces:

* persistent biodiversity pressures;
* habitat fragmentation;
* degraded watersheds;
* ecological disturbance;
* invasive species;
* climate-related changes;
* uneven restoration outcomes;
* fragmented governance;
* inconsistent performance measurement; and
* institutional-capacity challenges.

The central finding of this baseline is therefore:

> **The United States does not need to invent conservation from the beginning. It needs to determine which parts of the existing system are working, where capacity and ecological outcomes are falling short, and how successful institutions and practices can be strengthened, connected, and made durable.**

That distinction will guide the remainder of American Conservation Renewal.

The objective of the project is not to produce a list of everything government currently does for conservation.

It is to identify the **institutional conditions, policy mechanisms, and ecological interventions most capable of producing durable conservation outcomes.**

